Trace of the actual code:

  1. _executeInstantRedemption decodes PendleRedeemParams from redeemData and calls _redeemPT. Nothing in this function
  reads msg.sender, and nothing in this file grants any third party the right to pass redeemData — the
  caller/authorization path is entirely in the parent PendlePT / vault entry point, which is NOT shown. The claim's
  whole premise ("whenever redeemData is supplied by someone other than the position owner") is an assumption about
  unshown code, not a fact traced in the code under review.
  2. The first leg's limit: 0 is not an unguarded hole: the value flows entirely into sDAIAmount, which is immediately
  sDAI.redeem(...) into daiAmount, and daiAmount is then the amount of the second trade. So any value lost on leg one
  propagates one-for-one into leg two's output. The code's own comment states this and the control flow confirms it:
  both terminal branches apply the slippage check to the final amount —
    - asset != DAI: limit: params.minPurchaseAmount is passed into _executeTrade, which reverts if the output falls
  short;
    - asset == DAI: if (params.minPurchaseAmount > daiAmount) revert SlippageTooHigh(daiAmount,
  params.minPurchaseAmount);
  A sandwich of the hardcoded Curve pool shrinks sDAIAmount → daiAmount → assetsPurchased, and is therefore caught by
  the end-to-end guard. There is no path where leg-one loss escapes the leg-two/DAI-branch check. So the routing/limit:
  0 is not itself the defect.
  3. That means the exploit reduces entirely to "the guard value params.minPurchaseAmount is supplied by the caller, and
  the attacker sets it to 0." That is not a missing check in this contract; it is the caller choosing to waive the
  check that exists. For this to be a vulnerability, an unprivileged attacker must be able to (a) trigger redemption of
  someone else's position and (b) choose the redeem params. The finding asserts this via "approved spender / keeper /
  automated redemption" — but an approved spender and a keeper are, by definition, roles the owner or governance
  authorized, acting within their documented permission to set trade parameters; and no unprivileged-caller path is
  demonstrated in the shown code. Under the scope rule, exploitation that requires a trusted/authorized role supplying
  its own slippage parameter is out of scope.
  4. Also note deadline: block.timestamp is standard for atomic vault-internal trades (no pending-tx window exists — the
  trade executes in the same call), and _executeTrade routes through the Notional TradingModule, which is the component
  that owns oracle-based limit enforcement; nothing here bypasses it.

  Conclusion: the loss channel the finding names (hardcoded pool, limit: 0) is provably covered by the terminal
  minPurchaseAmount check on both branches, and the residual claim depends on an authorized party voluntarily setting
  its own slippage guard to zero via code that is not shown. Uncertainty and scope both resolve against the finding.

  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/staking/PendlePT_sUSDe.sol:_executeInstantRedemption:30|class=C15|Slippage from the hardcoded
  first leg propagates directly into daiAmount and is enforced end-to-end by params.minPurchaseAmount (via
  _executeTrade's limit, or the explicit revert SlippageTooHigh in the DAI branch), so the only way the exploit works is
  an authorized spender/keeper deliberately setting its own guard to zero — a trusted-role action via an entry point
  not present in the shown code.
